-{
-  "goal": "Implement 5 open Phase 7 items (7.5 cross-symptom correlation integration, 7.7 confidence calibration tests, 7.8 dedup consolidation tests/docs, 7.10 node resource investigation enhancements, 7.11 wire declarative template evidence rules into evidence collector) with TDD and documentation updates.",
-  "steps": [
-    {
-      "id": "step-1",
-      "title": "7.11a — Wire template evidenceRules into Hypothesis type and evidence collector (TDD: tests first)",
-      "description": "The template registry defines `evidenceRules` per hypothesis definition, but these rules are never consumed. The `Hypothesis` type in `hypothesis-types.ts` lacks an `evidenceRules` field, and the evidence collector in `hypothesis-evidence-collector.ts` never reads them — it uses hardcoded procedural collectors per category.\n\n**TDD tests first** in `src/investigators/__tests__/hypothesis-evidence-collector.test.ts`:\n1. Test that `collectEvidenceForHypothesis` for an `oom-kill` hypothesis finds OOMKilled evidence from container statuses and events (currently falls through to generic fallback).\n2. Test that a `generic-crash` hypothesis uses template evidence rules to match error/panic/fatal patterns in logs.\n3. Test that template-driven evidence collection produces supporting items matching rule patterns and contradicting items matching contradicting rule patterns.\n\n**Implementation:**\n1. Add `evidenceRules?: EvidenceRule[]` field to the `Hypothesis` interface in `src/investigators/hypothesis-types.ts`.\n2. In `src/investigators/hypothesis-generator.ts`, pass `evidenceRules` from the template definition through `buildHypothesis()` so they reach the `Hypothesis` object.\n3. In `src/investigators/hypothesis-evidence-collector.ts`, add a new `collectTemplateRuleEvidence(hypothesis, context)` function that iterates over `hypothesis.evidenceRules`, compiles each rule's pattern into a RegExp, and searches logs + events + finding titles for matches. Supporting rules produce supporting evidence items; contradicting rules produce contradicting items.\n4. In `collectEvidenceForHypothesis`, after the category-specific collector runs (or instead of the generic fallback), call `collectTemplateRuleEvidence` to augment the assessment with template-driven evidence. For categories WITH a dedicated collector (dns-failure, missing-secret, etc.), template rules act as a supplement. For categories WITHOUT a collector (oom-kill, generic-crash, bad-db-endpoint, missing-service), template rules ARE the primary evidence source.\n5. Update the `CATEGORY_COLLECTORS` dispatch table comment to document the fallback chain: dedicated collector → template rules → generic fallback.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-2",
-      "title": "7.11b — Add OOM-kill standalone template and missing category evidence collectors",
-      "description": "Currently `oom-kill` only exists as a sub-hypothesis under the `crashloop` template. There's no standalone OOM symptom template, so standalone OOM findings (container OOMKilled but NOT in CrashLoopBackOff) fall through to the generic template. Also, the `oom-kill` category has no dedicated evidence collector — it relies entirely on the generic fallback.\n\n**TDD tests first:**\n1. In `hypothesis-templates.test.ts`: test that a finding with title containing 'OOMKilled' matches a dedicated oom-kill template (not generic).\n2. In `hypothesis-evidence-collector.test.ts`: test that OOM-kill evidence collector finds OOMKilled container statuses, memory limits/requests from pod specs, and OOM-related events.\n3. In `hypothesis-evidence-collector.test.ts`: test `generic-crash` and `missing-service` categories use template rules for evidence (step-1 wiring).\n\n**Implementation:**\n1. Add an `oom-kill` template to `HYPOTHESIS_TEMPLATE_REGISTRY` in `hypothesis-templates.ts` with symptom patterns: `['oomkilled', 'oom kill', 'oom-kill', 'out of memory', 'exit code 137']`, symptomKeyPrefix `'pod-oom'`, and hypotheses: oom-kill (memory limit too low), config-error (memory leak from misconfigured caching), generic-crash (unbounded memory allocation). Each with appropriate evidenceRules.\n2. Add a `collectOOMKillEvidence` function in `hypothesis-evidence-collector.ts` that: scans container statuses for OOMKilled reason (current and last state), extracts memory limits and requests, collects OOM-related events, and checks logs for memory-related errors. Add to CATEGORY_COLLECTORS.\n3. Add `collectMissingServiceEvidence` function: check if referenced services exist in the Service inventory, check Endpoints for ready addresses. Add to CATEGORY_COLLECTORS.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-3",
-      "title": "7.10 — Enhance node resource investigation with capacity, taints, and pod correlation (TDD)",
-      "description": "Node conditions (NotReady, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure) and seeds already exist. What's missing: (a) node resource capacity vs allocatable analysis to detect over-committed nodes, (b) taint analysis to detect unschedulable taints that explain pod scheduling failures, (c) correlation between node pressure conditions and OOMKilled pods running on that node.\n\n**TDD tests first** in `src/investigators/__tests__/kubernetes-investigator.test.ts`:\n1. Test: Node with allocatable memory significantly lower than capacity → finding about node resource constraint.\n2. Test: Node with `NoSchedule` taint and pods stuck in Pending → causal edge linking taint to scheduling failure.\n3. Test: Node with MemoryPressure and pods on that node OOMKilled → causal edge from node pressure to OOM finding.\n4. Test: Healthy node (all conditions OK, no taints, normal capacity) → no additional findings.\n\nAlso add seeds tests in `seed-generator.test.ts`:\n5. Test: Node with taints that prevent scheduling generates a seed.\n\n**Implementation:**\n1. In `kubernetes-investigator.ts`, add `investigateNodeResources(context, counter)` function:\n   - Extract `status.allocatable` and `status.capacity` from Node resources.\n   - When allocatable memory < 80% of capacity, emit a `medium` finding about node resource constraints.\n   - Extract `spec.taints` from Node resources. For each `NoSchedule` or `NoExecute` taint, check if any Pending pods have matching tolerations. If a pod lacks a required toleration, emit a finding with a causal edge.\n2. In `investigateNodeConditions`, add correlation: when a node has MemoryPressure=True, scan for OOMKilled containers on pods assigned to that node (`spec.nodeName`). Create causal edges from the MemoryPressure finding to OOM findings.\n3. Wire `investigateNodeResources` into the public `investigateKubernetes` function.\n4. In `seed-generator.ts`, extend `seedsFromNodes` to also emit seeds for nodes with `NoSchedule` taints.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-4",
-      "title": "7.5 — Add orchestrator integration test for cross-symptom correlation E2E",
-      "description": "The `correlateAcrossSymptoms` function is implemented and unit-tested in `hypothesis-generator.test.ts`, and wired into the orchestrator. However, there's no integration test proving the full pipeline (seeds → investigators → hypothesis generation → cross-symptom correlation → meta-finding in final output) produces a cluster-wide meta-finding when multiple pods converge on the same root cause.\n\n**TDD tests** in `src/investigators/__tests__/orchestrator.test.ts`:\n1. Create a scenario with 3 pods in the same namespace, all in CrashLoopBackOff with DNS-failure evidence (logs containing 'no such host' for the same hostname). Verify that `runInvestigation` produces a `finding:meta:cluster-wide:dns-failure:*` meta-finding in the final sorted output.\n2. Verify the meta-finding's confidence is higher than any individual hypothesis finding's confidence (convergence boost).\n3. Verify the meta-finding's `affectedComponents` contains all 3 pod components.\n4. Create a scenario with 2 pods in different namespaces with the same DNS failure. Verify NO meta-finding is produced (namespace isolation).\n5. Verify the meta-finding survives the dedup passes (pass1 and pass2 don't accidentally remove it).\n\nThese are purely test additions — the implementation already exists. If any test fails, fix the integration wiring.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-5",
-      "title": "7.8 — Consolidate dedup: add contract tests, remove any remaining overlap, document architecture",
-      "description": "The orchestrator was refactored from 5 overlapping dedup layers to a cleaner 2-pass + suppression architecture. However, the contracts between passes need explicit test coverage and documentation to prevent future regressions.\n\n**TDD tests** in `src/investigators/__tests__/orchestrator.test.ts`:\n1. **Pass 1 contract test**: Given a heuristic finding and a hypothesis finding with the same (component, family), verify hypothesis displaces heuristic and absorbs its evidence.\n2. **Pass 1 contract test**: Given an AI finding and a hypothesis finding with the same (component, family), verify AI wins and hypothesis is dropped.\n3. **Pass 1 contract test**: Given two hypothesis findings with the same (component, family) but DIFFERENT titles (different root causes), verify both survive (they're competing theories).\n4. **Pass 2 contract test**: Given two findings with identical normalized titles but different components, verify dedup keeps the higher-confidence one.\n5. **Symptom suppression contract test**: Verify NotReady finding is suppressed when a root-cause hypothesis covers the same pod, but CrashLoopBackOff finding is always preserved.\n6. **Non-overlap test**: Verify that a finding processed by pass1 is not double-processed by pass2 in a way that loses information (e.g., evidence absorbed in pass1 is still present after pass2).\n\n**Implementation:**\n1. Review `normalizeIssueFamilyForPass1` and `normalizeTitleForPass2` for any patterns that overlap (both check `secret`+`missing` for example). If overlap exists, clarify contract: pass1 normalizes for component-merge, pass2 normalizes for title-dedup only.\n2. Add JSDoc contracts to `pass1ComponentMerge` and `pass2TitleDedup` documenting exactly what each pass is responsible for.\n3. Export `normalizeIssueFamilyForPass1` and `normalizeTitleForPass2` (or create test-visible wrappers) so the contract tests can verify normalization independently.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-6",
-      "title": "7.7 — Add confidence calibration unit tests and verify monotonicity programmatically",
-      "description": "The benchmark script (`scripts/benchmark-hypotheses.mjs`) already reports per-band accuracy and monotonicity checks. However, there are no unit-level tests that assert calibration properties as part of the test suite, and the scoring constants have not been verified against the test fixture corpus.\n\n**TDD tests** in `src/investigators/__tests__/hypothesis-ranker.test.ts`:\n1. **Monotonicity invariant test**: Create a series of hypotheses with increasing evidence quality (0 supporting → 1 generic → 1 specific → 2 specific + cross-source → 3 specific + cross-source + decisive). Assert that `scoreHypothesis` produces strictly increasing scores for each step. This encodes the monotonicity invariant as a regression test.\n2. **Confidence band boundary tests**: Assert that a hypothesis with no evidence scores 0.0. Assert that a hypothesis with only generic evidence scores ≤ 0.50 (GENERIC_ONLY_CAP). Assert that a hypothesis with maximum evidence (all bonuses, no penalties) scores ≤ 1.0.\n3. **Contradiction penalty test**: Given a hypothesis with 2 supporting and 1 contradicting item, verify the score is lower than the same hypothesis with 2 supporting and 0 contradicting.\n4. **Missing decisive penalty test**: Same pattern — verify missing-decisive items reduce the score.\n5. **Cross-scenario calibration test**: For each test fixture scenario (wrong-db-endpoint, missing-secret-crashloop, bad-image-tag, pvc-pending), construct the hypothesis + evidence that the pipeline would produce and verify the expected category gets the highest score. This makes the benchmark's `correct_top1` check into a unit test.\n\n**Implementation:**\n1. Add the tests above. Most should pass with current constants.\n2. If any monotonicity violations are found, adjust the scoring constants in `hypothesis-ranker.ts` (SUPPORTING_PER_ITEM, SPECIFICITY_PER_ITEM, etc.) to restore monotonicity.\n3. Add a `calibration` describe block grouping all calibration tests for easy identification.\n4. Update the benchmark script's comment to reference these unit tests as the primary calibration gate.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "step-7",
-      "title": "Update documentation to reflect all Phase 7 implementations",
-      "description": "Update all relevant documentation to mark Phase 7 items as resolved and document the new capabilities.\n\n**Files to update:**\n1. `docs/findings-and-improvements.md` — Add status markers to items 7.5, 7.7, 7.8, 7.10, 7.11 indicating they are now resolved. Add brief notes on what was implemented for each.\n2. `README.md` — Update the test count and feature list to reflect:\n   - Declarative hypothesis template registry with evidence rules\n   - Cross-symptom correlation producing cluster-wide meta-findings\n   - Node resource investigation (conditions, capacity, taints)\n   - Confidence calibration with monotonicity regression tests\n   - Consolidated 2-pass dedup architecture\n3. `MY_APPROACH_AND_THOUGHTS.md` — Add a paragraph about the Phase 7 enhancements: declarative templates replacing procedural code, the calibration approach, and the dedup consolidation rationale.\n\nNo code changes — documentation only. Build check ensures docs don't break anything.",
-      "requiredChecks": ["lint", "typecheck", "unit", "build"]
-    }
-  ]
-}
